WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.480
Starship Flight 15 targets the first catch of both stages next week. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 156. NASA is redesigning the lunar space suit. SpaceX is planning a starbase super compute site, and the engineering angle is Super Heavy's engine bay. Starship Flight 15 is targeted no earlier than November 13th from starbase. Keep track reports in a 75 minute window that opens at 4.45pm central time.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:34.520
Booster 22 and Ship 42, both Block 3 vehicles are assigned, and the super heavy booster is

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.600
due back at the tower about 6 minutes and 35 seconds after leaving the pad.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.040
The first catch attempt for a super heavy V3 vehicle.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:49.020
The ship will put 26 starling V3 satellites into a roughly 275 kilometer orbit, complete

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:54.740
14 orbits, and try a return catch only after systems checks confirm it is ready for reentry

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:55.760
and the landing burn.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.460
3 of those satellites carry cameras to inspect the heat shield.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:05.800
Every Starlink V3 satellite adds one terabit of capacity, a trillion bits per second, so

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.680
the set matches roughly 10 Falcon 9 launches of Starlink V2 Mini.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.560
A ship recovery would be the first time an upper stage has been caught after reaching orbital

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.180
velocity.

00:01:16.181 --> 00:01:21.421
NASA is altering its lunar space suit design because of Artemis 4 schedule pressure, ours

00:01:21.421 --> 00:01:26.921
technical reports, two months after the prior update on the same topic. NASA is shifting

00:01:26.921 --> 00:01:31.621
the approach to keep the overall lunar landing timeline intact. The adjustment addresses

00:01:31.621 --> 00:01:35.901
concerns that the original suit development path would slip the crude landing target.

00:01:36.421 --> 00:01:40.801
The redesign aims to reduce development risk without altering the core requirements for

00:01:40.801 --> 00:01:46.628
lunar surface operations. Near the port of Brown'sville, SpaceX plans a $24 billion

00:01:46.628 --> 00:01:52.768
dollar super compute center and gas plant, crypto briefing reports, on a 444 acre site.

00:01:53.288 --> 00:01:59.388
The facility would use as many as 35 temporary gas turbines for an initial 748 megawatts

00:01:59.388 --> 00:01:59.868
of output.

00:02:00.508 --> 00:02:04.888
A December public hearing is scheduled on tax abatements and economic development agreements.

00:02:05.508 --> 00:02:09.688
The site already has applications for mass grading and air quality permits covering

00:02:09.688 --> 00:02:10.328
the turbines.

00:02:11.128 --> 00:02:15.428
Job postings reference the Starbase supercomputer and its on-site generation model.

00:02:15.428 --> 00:02:21.108
The project forms part of a broader push toward 13 gigawatts of total AI compute capacity

00:02:21.108 --> 00:02:22.908
by the end of 2027.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:28.870
Elon Musk said SpaceX's anthropic compute contract works both ways on termination,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:33.490
Tess Lerotti reports, because either party can exit on 90 days notice.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:41.130
The contract runs through May 2029 at $1.25 billion per month, and the same termination

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:46.930
terms appear in later SpaceX compute deals with Google and reflection AI. Musk noted that

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:52.670
if compute becomes plentiful and cheap, SpaceX revenue would drop, and he emphasized symmetry

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:56.550
so no party should expect one-sided gains if market conditions change.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:02.170
Political shifts in Texas, including the incoming governor's call for a data center moratorium,

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:05.230
formed the backdrop for recent discussion of the clause.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:11.805
quarterly SpaceX revenue is projected to exceed NASA's full fiscal 2027 budget

00:03:11.805 --> 00:03:18.545
request. Trading view reports, topping the $18.8 billion NASA figure. NASA also

00:03:18.545 --> 00:03:22.945
selected Starship to launch lunar communications satellites in 2028.

00:03:23.645 --> 00:03:28.805
Deutsche Bank reaffirmed its buy rating on SPCX, citing political retaliation

00:03:28.805 --> 00:03:34.205
risks as larger than near-term business risks. SpaceX plans to begin launching and

00:03:34.205 --> 00:03:40.385
Nvidia co-designed Star Mind Computers in the 3rd quarter of 2027, with deployment ramping

00:03:40.385 --> 00:03:42.825
in 2028 and 2029.

00:03:43.932 --> 00:03:50.012
Vandenberg Space Force Base is set for a Falcon 9 liftoff Monday evening, KSBY reports,

00:03:50.572 --> 00:03:51.572
on the current manifest.

00:03:52.152 --> 00:03:56.332
That flight continues yesterday's California record, 2026 cadence.

00:03:57.396 --> 00:04:02.616
Five people stranded in snowy mountain terrain in Kazakhstan were reached because Starlink provided

00:04:02.616 --> 00:04:09.236
connectivity where no cell towers existed, Tesla Roddy reports. Elon Musk highlighted the incident

00:04:09.236 --> 00:04:15.736
as showing the system's value for emergency response in remote areas. SpaceX is no longer selling

00:04:15.736 --> 00:04:20.596
commercial starship launches, according to a post on X, and there is no clear path for

00:04:20.596 --> 00:04:25.536
traditional payloads on the vehicle at present. The shift reflects internal priorities around

00:04:25.536 --> 00:04:31.848
rapid iteration and starlink deployment rather than external commercial sales. One thing worth

00:04:31.848 --> 00:04:36.748
watching is that NASA's late lunar space suit redesign has made suit development the pacing

00:04:36.748 --> 00:04:42.268
item for Artemis IV, because any further iteration risks pushing the first crude landing beyond

00:04:42.268 --> 00:04:47.488
the current target date. Hardware qualification timelines remain difficult to compress even

00:04:47.488 --> 00:04:52.448
when the launch vehicle itself is ready, and historical parallels with earlier NASA suit

00:04:52.448 --> 00:04:57.528
programs show repeated schedule pressure from certification requirements that cannot be fully

00:04:57.528 --> 00:05:03.571
parallelize with vehicle development. On the AI front, SpaceX plans to lease data center

00:05:03.571 --> 00:05:08.831
capacity to other companies before Grock needs it while outlining AI chip facilities in

00:05:08.831 --> 00:05:15.051
Texas. The company is tracking toward an annualized revenue rate above $100 billion in December,

00:05:15.471 --> 00:05:23.171
with a best guess of $500 billion by December 2027, driven in part by AI infrastructure. Musk

00:05:23.171 --> 00:05:28.691
stated that SpaceX is getting $50 billion a year for $35 billion investments, and can

00:05:28.691 --> 00:05:31.151
bring a data center online in eight or nine months.

00:05:31.691 --> 00:05:36.971
The anthropic contract alone represents nearly $45 billion over its term at the current

00:05:36.971 --> 00:05:37.291
rate.

00:05:38.205 --> 00:05:43.185
If you want the engineering angle on why Super Heavy carries 33 Raptors, start with the

00:05:43.185 --> 00:05:44.185
mass it has to lift.

00:05:44.745 --> 00:05:49.605
The booster is a roughly 5,000 ton vehicle that has to leave the pad with engine out margin.

00:05:49.605 --> 00:05:54.785
The ability to lose an engine and still fly, then shut down a subset of chambers for

00:05:54.785 --> 00:05:59.785
boost back, the burn that turns it toward the tower, and for the landing burn, without

00:05:59.785 --> 00:06:01.045
losing control authority.

00:06:01.605 --> 00:06:08.025
Each Raptor 3 makes between 230 and 250 metric tons of thrust at sea level, so the full

00:06:08.025 --> 00:06:11.185
set supplies the 18 million pounds required at lift-off.

00:06:11.685 --> 00:06:18.925
A back-of-envelope figure, 33 times 240 metric tons is about 7,900 metric tons of thrust

00:06:18.925 --> 00:06:24.225
on a 5,000 ton stack, a lift-off thrust to weight on the order of 1.6.

00:06:24.885 --> 00:06:29.245
Many smaller chambers avoid the combustion instability and fabrication limits that can

00:06:29.245 --> 00:06:35.725
strain single chamber designs, such as Saturn 5's F1, which made on the order of 1.5 million

00:06:35.725 --> 00:06:41.165
pounds each. Five of them on that first stage. High volume production of smaller engines

00:06:41.165 --> 00:06:47.305
is how per unit cost falls, while turbo machinery and chamber materials iterate quickly. Super

00:06:47.305 --> 00:06:51.525
Heavy is 9 meters wide and 72.3 meters tall in Block 3.

00:06:52.045 --> 00:06:57.285
With liquid oxygen and liquid methane tanks split by a common bulkhead, one shared wall,

00:06:57.565 --> 00:07:03.625
the same approach Saturn V used on the S2 and S4B stages, keeping the center of mass manageable

00:07:03.625 --> 00:07:04.805
on a scent and return.

00:07:05.525 --> 00:07:10.385
That common wall saves the mass of a second dome at the price of more plumbing and a harder

00:07:10.385 --> 00:07:13.245
tank to tank inspection path, which is the trade.

00:07:13.245 --> 00:07:19.245
Raptor is the first full-flow stage combustion engine to reach flight. Both turbo pumps driven

00:07:19.245 --> 00:07:27.125
by pre-burner gas, burning methane and oxygen at 650 kg per second. 33 at full throttle is on the

00:07:27.125 --> 00:07:35.305
order of 21,000 kg of propellant every second. Vacuum versions reach 258 to 275 metric tons.

00:07:35.925 --> 00:07:41.205
A landing burn cannot use the full ring because on a light booster that thrust would be many times

00:07:41.205 --> 00:07:45.945
the remaining weight, so the design shuts down to a subset and keeps control authority.

00:07:46.565 --> 00:07:50.505
The layout trades added plumbing for the ability to lose multiple engines and

00:07:50.505 --> 00:07:55.985
still complete the profile, which is why 33 became the baseline rather than a few larger units.

00:07:57.105 --> 00:08:03.497
SPCX ran higher by a little over 8%. Before we go, Monday evening at Vandenberg is the

00:08:03.497 --> 00:08:08.037
next timed launch on the board, and that's a wrap on today's SpaceX developments.

